### What is the Course Schedule Cycle Check problem?

The problem gives a number of courses and a list of prerequisite pairs [a, b] meaning course a requires course b first. The task is to determine whether all courses can be completed, which is possible only if the dependency graph has no cycle. If a cycle exists, no valid ordering exists.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;

public class CourseScheduleCycleCheck {
    private static final int WHITE = 0, GREY = 1, BLACK = 2;

    public boolean canFinish(int numCourses, int[][] prerequisites) {
        List<List<Integer>> adj = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < numCourses; i++) adj.add(new ArrayList<>());
        for (int[] p : prerequisites) adj.get(p[1]).add(p[0]);

        int[] colour = new int[numCourses];
        for (int i = 0; i < numCourses; i++) {
            if (colour[i] == WHITE && hasCycle(i, adj, colour)) return false;
        }
        return true;
    }

    private boolean hasCycle(int node, List<List<Integer>> adj, int[] colour) {
        colour[node] = GREY;
        for (int next : adj.get(node)) {
            if (colour[next] == GREY) return true;  // back edge: cycle
            if (colour[next] == WHITE && hasCycle(next, adj, colour)) return true;
        }
        colour[node] = BLACK;
        return false;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        CourseScheduleCycleCheck solver = new CourseScheduleCycleCheck();
        System.out.println("No cycle: " + solver.canFinish(2, new int[][]{{1, 0}}));
        System.out.println("Cycle: " + solver.canFinish(2, new int[][]{{1, 0}, {0, 1}}));
    }
}
```

```text
No cycle: true
Cycle: false
```

### Why use three colours instead of a simple visited set?

A simple visited set cannot distinguish between a node that is currently on the recursion stack (an ancestor) and a node that was fully explored on a different path. In a directed graph, reaching a fully explored node is not a cycle, it is a cross edge. Only reaching a node that is currently on the recursion stack (grey) is a back edge that indicates a cycle. The third colour (black) lets us skip fully explored nodes safely.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class WhyThreeColours {
    // Two-colour approach (visited/not visited):
    //   - Cannot distinguish ancestor (back edge) from cross edge
    //   - Would falsely report a cycle on a diamond-shaped DAG
    //
    // Three-colour approach:
    //   WHITE = not visited
    //   GREY  = on current recursion stack (ancestor)
    //   BLACK = fully explored, safe to skip

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Diamond DAG: 0->1, 0->2, 1->3, 2->3
        // Two-colour: visiting 3 from 1, then from 2 looks like a cycle (WRONG)
        // Three-colour: 3 is BLACK after path 1->3, so path 2->3 skips it (CORRECT)
        System.out.println("Three colours distinguish back edges from cross edges.");
    }
}
```

### How does the DFS colouring algorithm work step by step?

When we enter a node, we colour it grey (on the stack). We then visit each neighbour. If a neighbour is grey, we found a back edge and report a cycle. If a neighbour is white, we recurse into it. If a neighbour is black, we skip it. After all neighbours are processed, we colour the node black (fully explored).

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;

public class DFSColouringTrace {
    private static final int WHITE = 0, GREY = 1, BLACK = 2;

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Graph: 0->1, 1->2, 2->0 (cycle)
        List<List<Integer>> adj = List.of(
            List.of(1), List.of(2), List.of(0)
        );
        int[] colour = {WHITE, WHITE, WHITE};

        System.out.println("Enter 0: colour=GREY");
        colour[0] = GREY;
        System.out.println("  Enter 1: colour=GREY");
        colour[1] = GREY;
        System.out.println("    Enter 2: colour=GREY");
        colour[2] = GREY;
        System.out.println("      Neighbour 0 is GREY => CYCLE DETECTED!");
    }
}
```

```text
Enter 0: colour=GREY
  Enter 1: colour=GREY
    Enter 2: colour=GREY
      Neighbour 0 is GREY => CYCLE DETECTED!
```

### How is this different from Kahn's in-degree BFS?

| Feature | DFS colouring | Kahn's BFS |
|---|---|---|
| Approach | recursive DFS with three colours | iterative BFS with in-degree counting |
| Cycle detection | grey neighbour = back edge | processed count < V = cycle |
| Output | boolean (cycle exists) | topological order + boolean |
| Stack usage | O(V) recursion depth | O(V) queue |
| Implementation | recursive, 3-state array | iterative, in-degree array + queue |

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;

public class KahnsAlgorithm {
    public boolean canFinish(int n, int[][] prerequisites) {
        List<List<Integer>> adj = new ArrayList<>();
        int[] inDegree = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) adj.add(new ArrayList<>());
        for (int[] p : prerequisites) {
            adj.get(p[1]).add(p[0]);
            inDegree[p[0]]++;
        }

        Queue<Integer> queue = new LinkedList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (inDegree[i] == 0) queue.add(i);
        }

        int processed = 0;
        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            int node = queue.poll();
            processed++;
            for (int next : adj.get(node)) {
                if (--inDegree[next] == 0) queue.add(next);
            }
        }
        return processed == n; // if not, cycle exists
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Kahn's: " + new KahnsAlgorithm().canFinish(3, new int[][]{{1,0},{2,1}}));
    }
}
```

```text
Kahn's: true
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include no prerequisites (no edges, no cycle), a single course, self-loops (course requires itself), and disconnected components where a cycle exists in only one component.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class CycleEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        CourseScheduleCycleCheck solver = new CourseScheduleCycleCheck();

        // No prerequisites: always possible
        System.out.println("No prereqs: " + solver.canFinish(3, new int[][]{}));

        // Self-loop: course 0 requires course 0
        System.out.println("Self-loop: " + solver.canFinish(1, new int[][]{{0, 0}}));

        // Disconnected: cycle in one component
        System.out.println("Disconnected cycle: " + solver.canFinish(4,
            new int[][]{{1, 0}, {2, 3}, {3, 2}}));
    }
}
```

```text
No prereqs: true
Self-loop: false
Disconnected cycle: false
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is using only two colours (visited/unvisited) in a directed graph, which falsely reports cycles on diamond-shaped DAGs. Another mistake is not starting DFS from every unvisited node, which misses cycles in disconnected components.

Wrong code:

```java
// WRONG: two colours only, false positive on diamond DAG
boolean[] visited = new boolean[n];
private boolean hasCycleBad(int node) {
    if (visited[node]) return true; // BUG: black nodes also return true
    visited[node] = true;
    for (int next : adj.get(node)) {
        if (hasCycleBad(next)) return true;
    }
    return false;
}
```

Correct code:

```java
// CORRECT: three colours distinguish grey from black
private boolean hasCycleGood(int node, int[] colour) {
    colour[node] = GREY;
    for (int next : adj.get(node)) {
        if (colour[next] == GREY) return true;  // back edge
        if (colour[next] == WHITE && hasCycleGood(next, colour)) return true;
    }
    colour[node] = BLACK;
    return false;
}
```

### When should you use this technique?

Use three-colour DFS for cycle detection in directed graphs where you need a boolean answer. If you also need the topological order, Kahn's BFS is more natural because it produces the order as a side effect. Both run in O(V + E) time.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class WhenToUseDFSColouring {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Use DFS colouring when:");
        System.out.println("  - Need boolean cycle detection in directed graph.");
        System.out.println("  - Natural recursive implementation.");
        System.out.println("Use Kahn's BFS when:");
        System.out.println("  - Need the topological order as output.");
        System.out.println("  - Prefer iterative implementation.");
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer knows that the grey colour is exactly the recursion stack membership, and that this is what distinguishes a back edge (cycle) from a cross edge (safe) or forward edge (safe) in a directed graph. They also know that in undirected graphs, you only need to check that you do not revisit the parent, because every edge back to a visited non-parent node is a cycle. In production at National Bank of India, the workflow engine validates that approval step dependencies form a DAG before saving the workflow, using three-colour DFS to catch circular dependencies immediately.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class SeniorCycleNotes {
    // Edge classification in directed DFS:
    //   Tree edge:    white -> grey (parent to child)
    //   Back edge:    any -> grey (cycle!)
    //   Forward edge: any -> black (ancestor to descendant, safe)
    //   Cross edge:   any -> black (between branches, safe)

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Grey = on recursion stack => back edge => cycle.");
        System.out.println("Black = fully explored => cross/forward edge => safe.");
        System.out.println("Undirected: just check parent, no three colours needed.");
    }
}
```
